   "subject": "Re: memory problem",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">Hi Gilbert,<div><br></div><div>I think if you can install from our devel branch on github it will do better.  You can do that by</div><div><p style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">library(devtools)<br>install_github(&quot;nimble-dev/nimble&quot;, ref = &quot;devel&quot;, subdir = &quot;packages/nimble&quot;)<br>(unfortunately that seems to install some packages that are suggests but not imports).</p><div>Your model is still large due to all the latent occupancy states, so the building will still be slow.  In the attached file I’ve shown some steps you can take to improve it by vectorizing some declarations and using a user-defined distribution to avoid some of the BUGS code constructions you had.  One could go further and use more effective samplers for the z[i,j] latent states or cut them completely out of the model by writing a user-defined distribution that directly sums over them.  User-defined functions and distributions are in section 5.2.5 of the user manual.  Let us know if we can support you in that.</div><div><br></div><div>Perry</div><div><br></div><div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
